    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean": {
      "title": "Academic Dean's Office",
      "description": "Academic Dean's Office | Pomona College in Claremont, California - Pomona College Skip to main content Main Navigation Admissions & Aid Academics Life @ Pomona News & Events About Alumni & Families Give today Quicklinks & Search Menu Close Main navigation Admissions & Aid Academics Life @ Pomona News & Events About Alumni & Families Search Go Quicklinks Quicklinks A-Z Directory Athletics Campus Map Catalog Dining Menus Give Today Pages for... Audience Navigation New Students Students Faculty Staff Quicklinks & Search Search Go Quicklinks Quicklinks (Mobile) A-Z Directory Athletics Campus Map Catalog Dining Menus Give Today Pages for... Pages For… New Students Students Faculty Staff Close Menu You are here Home Administration Academic Dean's Office Academic Dean's Office New Faculty Orientation 2026 Pomona College welcomes new faculty! To help support your success, we are organizing New Faculty Orientation , a two-day event on August 24 and 25. This will be an opportunity to meet each other, build community, and engage with current faculty and staff. Click here for more information, updates, and links. Welcome to Pomona College! The Dean of College's office is at the heart of Pomona's mission, dedicated to the shaping and sustaining of its intellectual life. We support the faculty in their teaching and research, administer the academic program and help the College explore new instructional initiatives. We also support cooperative programs with our counterparts at the other Claremont Colleges. On this website, you can find information about applying for research and teaching grants, planning sabbaticals, recruiting new faculty, and a host of other topics. Learn more about the Dean's Staff and find out who we are and who can help you with what. And we encourage you not just to explore the web page, but also to call, e-mail or, best of all, stop by to see us on the second floor of Alexander Hall. Click here for the Faculty Handbook . Important dates, events, and meeting times for chairs can be found here . The calendar is updated regularly. Academic Advising Academic advising at Pomona begins before students register for courses for the first time and continues throughout the undergraduate experience. The Academic Dean’s Office coordinates initial advising assignments for all incoming Pomona students and collaborates with other campus offices to make advising resources available to faculty advisors and students alike. The Academic Dean’s Office also assists faculty advisors and students with questions about advising, advising resources, and advising assignments. Academic Dean's Office Office A-Z Information, General Policies & Forms Internal Funding for Research & Pedagogy Human Subjects Research (IRB) Sponsored Research Teaching, Learning & Advising Student Academic Success Faculty Development Resources for Chairs Statements/Messages Diversity Our Staff Contact Us Kerry Roccia Executive Assistant to the Vice President for Academic Affairs and Dean of the College; Academic Affairs Office Operations Manager Alexander Hall kerry.roccia@pomona.edu (909) 621-8137 Mailing Address Pomona College 333 N. College Way Claremont , CA 91711 Get in touch Contact (909) 621-8000 Contact Us Connect Social media  links Instagram Twitter Tiktok LinkedIn Youtube Facebook Footer The Arts Athletics Accessibility Location & Maps Emergency Employment Social Media Directory TCCS Give back to Pomona Support Pomona Part of The Claremont Colleges Legal ©2026 Pomona College Privacy Nondiscrimination Feedback"
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32243": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, Department of Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, Department of Biology Position ID: Pomona-Department of Biology-AP [#32243] Position Title: Assistant Professor of Biology Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Plant Biology / Plant Pathology Appl Deadline: 2026/10/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/03 , updated 2026/06/24 , listed until 2026/10/01) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Biology is hiring a tenure track assistant professor of biology to join the department in July 2027. We are seeking a plant biologist in any area of plant biology, however those with an interest in plant physiology are especially encouraged to apply. We are looking for candidates with a demonstrated interest in teaching and conducting research with undergraduate students. The successful candidate will offer an upper-level course in Plant Physiology or other upper-level plant biology course with laboratory, and teach an introductory course with laboratory. Pomona College is a private, undergraduate, selective liberal arts institution enrolling approximately 1,700 students. Pomona College is the oldest and founding member of the Claremont Colleges, a consortium of institutions that include five undergraduate colleges and two graduate universities, located in southern California. Intercollegiate collaboration among the colleges provides university-scale services and facilities, including the Bernard Field Station and the Claremont Colleges Libraries. While each institution is independent, undergraduate students can take courses at any of the colleges and research collaboration among faculty is encouraged through professional development networks. Pomona faculty in the Environmental Analysis Department partner regularly with colleagues in and beyond Claremont. The faculty in Biology welcome applicants who are committed to high quality, innovative undergraduate teaching and to mentoring undergraduate student research. Pomona College’s plant growth facility includes greenhouses, growth chambers, and a headhouse for planting, preparation, and storage, with a full-time dedicated greenhouse manager (more detailed information on the facilities is available). The College actively promotes a dynamic and inclusive environment in which students and employees of diverse backgrounds, cultures, and perspectives can learn and work. Pomona College is an equal opportunity employer that welcomes members of all groups and reaffirms its commitment not to discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, gender, age, religion, caste, gender identity or expression, disability, or sexual orientation in its educational programs, activities, and employment practices. In recent years, over 51% of entering classes identified as students of color, 23% as first-generation, 14% international, and nearly 20% as Pell eligible students. Recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty committed to building an inclusive community is vital to educating an equally diverse student body. Candidates who can contribute to this goal are particularly encouraged to apply. Preferred Qualifications: \r\nA Ph.D. in a field of biology by the start of employment and expertise in plant biology is required. Teaching experience; a demonstrated commitment to undergraduate teaching and research; and a commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, in biology are also priorities. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in a field of biology by the start of employment and expertise in plant biology. Application Deadline: (at 11:59 PM Pacific Time): 10/01/2026 Open Until Filled: Yes Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit to Academic Jobs Online #32243 1) a cover letter; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a statement demonstrating commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, in environmental science; 4) a statement of teaching principles (3 pages maximum); 5) a research statement (3 pages maximum); and 6) graduate transcripts. As part of the application process, all candidates must provide contact information for at least three references who can submit a confidential letter of recommendation; however, only the references of short-listed candidates (those invited to a phone/Zoom interview) will be asked to submit a letter. All application materials should be posted by Thursday, October 1 at 11:59 pm PDT. You must meet the minimum qualifications to be considered for this position. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $130,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training For any questions, please contact Dr. E. J. Crane, ej.crane@pomona.edu. When will references be contacted to provide a letter of recommendation? When candidate is invited to phone interview. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Diversity Statement Graduate School transcript(s) Two references (actual letters acceptable but not required, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.biology.pomona.edu/index.shtml Department of Biology 175 W 6th St Claremont, Ca 91711 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/faculty-jobs/how-prepare-diversity-statement": {
      "title": "Preparing your Equity and Inclusion Statement",
      "description": "Preparing your Equity and Inclusion Statement | Pomona College in Claremont, California - Pomona College Skip to main content Main Navigation Admissions & Aid Academics Life @ Pomona News & Events About Alumni & Families Give today Quicklinks & Search Menu Close Main navigation Admissions & Aid Academics Life @ Pomona News & Events About Alumni & Families Search Go Quicklinks Quicklinks A-Z Directory Athletics Campus Map Catalog Dining Menus Give Today Pages for... Audience Navigation New Students Students Faculty Staff Quicklinks & Search Search Go Quicklinks Quicklinks (Mobile) A-Z Directory Athletics Campus Map Catalog Dining Menus Give Today Pages for... Pages For… New Students Students Faculty Staff Close Menu You are here Home Administration Academic Dean's Office Faculty Jobs How to Prepare a Diversity Statement Preparing your Equity and Inclusion Statement From the Pomona College Faculty Handbook, 2022-2023 (pp. 175-176) : “In 1993, addressing student and faculty concerns about the need for greater diversity in the College’s curriculum, admissions, faculty, staff, and administrative hiring, the faculty passed the following resolution, declaring: ‘Increased ethnic diversity among the faculty would enable Pomona College to pursue its educational mission more effectively, and additional efforts to achieve diversity are warranted.’ In 2004, the faculty reaffirms this resolution and rededicates itself to seeking excellence and diversity in all searches at the College.” One of the most critical components of your application dossier is your Equity and Inclusion Statement. The Equity and Inclusion Statement is an opportunity to share with the search committee and with the department your values, commitments, and abilities to contribute to Pomona’s educational mission. Should you become a faculty member, the statement can also serve as a “first record” of your journey with DEI as an instructor, researcher, mentor, and community member. The statement should emphasize your knowledge about the sources of inequalities in higher education, especially at historically white institutions such as Pomona College, and your track record and success in mentoring, teaching, and engaging with under-represented/marginalized groups on and beyond campus. Finally, you can use the statement to explain how your experiences and skills with teaching, mentoring, and advising through a diversity, equity, and inclusive framework align with or challenge Pomona’s DEI values and will contribute to growing inclusive excellence across the campus, from curricular to the co-curricular to residential life. For more information about Pomona College: Pomona College Facts at a Glance Lighting the Path: A Vision for Diversity Pomona College Institute for Inclusive Excellence The Strategic Vision for Pomona College Global Pomona Project Please feel free to use any of the questions below (or a combination of them) to help focus writing your statement. Your statement may or may not address all of these questions and you are certainly welcome to mention anything you think missing in this invitation to reflect on your teaching, research, and advising. If you have any additional questions, please contact Associate Dean April Mayes ( april.mayes@pomona.edu ) for further assistance. Getting Started A good place to start thinking about your approach to equity and inclusion in the academy may be to reflect on your values, motivations, and aspirations, including the ways in which your journey to this point has been influenced by family, mentors, instructors, staff members, and other scholars. This first step might help clarify what diversity, equity, and inclusion means to you, given your life history and professional experiences, and the frames of reference you have used to develop strategies in your teaching, research, service, and advising that contributed to DEI. Some Prompts Teaching Based on research conducted among Pomona faculty, the Institute for Inclusive Excellence (IIE) developed a working (and dynamic) definition of inclusive teaching: “Inclusive teaching builds community, teaches critical content, connects to students’ lives and interests, is accessible to all students, and cares for the whole student.” Provide a reflection about this statement. Describe how diversity, equity, inclusion, and access inform how you will craft assignments, class requirements, the structure of your syllabi, the design of your learning management sites (Blackboard, Canvas, etc.), and student participation in your classes. What do you do as an instructor to consider DEI in the development of your courses? How do you consider positionality and power within the classroom and/or within your discipline? Do you include the research not produced within the U.S. academy and/or indigenous methodologies and knowledge systems? Research Has your research involved collaboration with diverse groups of colleagues and/or interface with community-based partners? What did you gain from that experience and how did it inform your scholarship? Does your research address diversity, equity, and inclusion? How so? Does your scholarship focus on any marginalized group? If so, describe the interpersonal and professional dynamics you have addressed conducting this kind of research. Do you anticipate sharing your insights in the classroom? With colleagues? How does your research address the key assumptions of your discipline? Do you include knowledge produced outside the U.S. or European academies in your work? Advising and Mentoring a Diverse Student Body Have you advised students and/or served as a mentor for student organizations who are from marginalized groups or who have little representation on their campuses? How did you approach this work and what did you learn from the experience? Do these lessons from advising inform your teaching or research in any way? What is your plan for helping recruit and retain students in your discipline? How will you encourage first-generation students, for example, to become involved in your field/discipline, in international study or domestic exchange? Discuss your experience working with international students. Academic Dean's Office Office A-Z Information, General Policies & Forms Faculty Jobs Leaves Faculty Committees Accreditation Access to Course Essentials (ACE) ITS Purchasing Visiting Scholars Travel Tracker Instructions Internal Funding for Research & Pedagogy Human Subjects Research (IRB) Sponsored Research Teaching, Learning & Advising Student Academic Success Faculty Development Resources for Chairs Statements/Messages Diversity Our Staff Contact Us Kerry Roccia Executive Assistant to the Vice President for Academic Affairs and Dean of the College; Academic Affairs Office Operations Manager Alexander Hall kerry.roccia@pomona.edu (909) 621-8137 Mailing Address Pomona College 333 N. College Way Claremont , CA 91711 Get in touch Contact (909) 621-8000 Contact Us Connect Social media  links Instagram Twitter Tiktok LinkedIn Youtube Facebook Footer The Arts Athletics Accessibility Location & Maps Emergency Employment Social Media Directory TCCS Give back to Pomona Support Pomona Part of The Claremont Colleges Legal ©2026 Pomona College Privacy Nondiscrimination Feedback"
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32241": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, Computer Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, Computer Science Position ID: Pomona-Computer Science-ASSTPROF [#32241] Position Title: Assistant Professor of Computer Science Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Computer Science / All areas Appl Deadline: 2026/10/04 23:59:59  ( accepting applications posted 2026/07/01 , updated 2026/06/24 , listed until 2026/10/12) Position Description: Apply Position Description Pomona College seeks applications for an Assistant Professor of Computer Science to begin on July 1, 2027. We seek candidates with a strong commitment to i) inclusive undergraduate teaching across all levels of our curriculum; ii) mentoring a diverse student body and fostering a supportive learning environment; iii) developing a strong, independent research program that involves undergraduates and leads to peer-reviewed publications; and iv) helping build community within our Department and the College. We welcome applicants with broad intellectual interests from a wide range of subdisciplines, with a preference for candidates whose research and teaching interests complement those of the current faculty ( Computer Science Department Website ) or who can contribute to expanding the department's curricular range and scholarly interests. The teaching commitment is four courses per year over two semesters of instruction (2-2). CS faculty are expected to teach at least two required courses among our introductory (introduction to programming and computer science, mathematical foundations, and data structures) and/or core (algorithms, languages and theory of computation, and computer systems) sequences. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in hand by their start date, usually July 1, 2027. Our department values experience working with undergraduate students from diverse backgrounds and a commitment to improving higher education for underrepresented students through teaching, scholarship, or service. After graduation, recent CS majors have taken jobs in industry and national research labs, and have pursued graduate programs at top research labs. Pomona College, located in Southern California, is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also includes Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, and Claremont Graduate University. Students can cross-register for courses at any of the colleges, which allows Pomona students to enjoy the advantages of personalized instruction and close faculty-student interaction combined with the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona's Computer Science department currently has 10 full-time, tenured or tenure-track faculty members, and there are over 30 CS faculty across the Claremont Colleges. In collaboration with the Claremont College Consortium, Pomona College offers a variety of professional development, mentorship, and networking opportunities for junior faculty, including the opportunity for entering faculty members to apply for a fully funded leave in their fourth year at the College. A complete application includes a cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts, three brief statements–one addressing the candidate’s teaching philosophy, one addressing the candidate’s scholarship, and one addressing the candidate’s ability to mentor a diverse student bod y and three or more letters of reference, at least one of which evaluates the candidate's teaching. Please upload electronic copies of all materials to Academic Jobs Online #32241. Review of applications will begin on October 4, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $135,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . For further information, we can be reached via email at cssearch@pomona.edu . Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Diversity Statement Graduate transcripts Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.cs.pomona.edu Computer Science Department Pomona College 185 E Sixth St Claremont, CA 91711 USA © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32246": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, English Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, English Position ID: Pomona-English-AP [#32246] Position Title: Assistant Professor of English Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Latinx Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/07/01 , updated 2026/06/24 , listed until 2026/12/24) Position Description: Apply Position Description The English Department at Pomona College invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor to begin Fall 2027. At a moment when the department is in conversation about diversifying the major as well as building on its current strengths, we seek a colleague whose research focuses on US-Latinx literature and culture. We are open to various areas of expertise. Transnational, hemispheric, and/or comparative approaches welcome. Areas of interest may include but are not limited to: decolonial solidarity frameworks, environmental and digital humanities, migration, border and critical refugee studies, gender and sexuality, performance, aesthetics, global South, and considerations of the more-than-human. We seek to attract, develop, and retain faculty that contribute to the cultural, linguistic, experiential, and diverse curricular offerings of the department.\r\n \r\nThe teaching load at Pomona College is two courses per semester. Faculty are expected to teach across all levels of the undergraduate curriculum and are eligible for generous teaching and research grants. Candidates should have Ph.D. in hand at time of appointment. Complete application includes: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) graduate transcripts, (d) three brief, 1-page statements – one addressing teaching philosophy and experience, one addressing scholarship and future research directions, and one addressing demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (e) three letters of recommendations (f) writing sample 20pp. Please apply through https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32246 \">Academic Jobs Online #32246 . Review of applications begins October 15, 2026.\r\n \r\nPomona College is a highly selective private liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1750 students, all undergraduates, and a student-faculty ratio of eight to one. The College and the Department support equal access to higher education and value working in a richly diverse environment. Of the students admitted into the Class of 2024, 58.8% self-identify as domestic students of color, 13.6% international and 20.7% are first generation college students. The successful candidate will have the ability to teach and mentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds–utilizing pedagogical practices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Pomona is the founding member of The Claremont Colleges, a unique constellation of five small undergraduate colleges and two graduate institutions located in beautiful Southern California near the foothills of the San Gabriel mountains, about 35 miles east of Los Angeles. Students of the Claremont Colleges can cross-register for courses at any of the colleges and have access to a shared library system containing over 2 million volumes. Pomona students thus enjoy the advantages of personalized instruction and close faculty-student interaction in a small liberal arts college setting, combined with the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $125,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. For any questions, please contact Dr. Cherene Sherrard-Johnson, Search Committee Chair and Department Chair, cherene.sherrard-johnson@pomona.edu . Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Diversity Two Graduate School transcripts Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.pomona.edu/academics/departments/english 140 W. 6th Street Crookshank Hall #106 Claremont, CA 91711-6335 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32240": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, Linguistics and Cognitive Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, Linguistics and Cognitive Science Position ID: Pomona-Linguistics and Cognitive Science-APCOGSCI [#32240] Position Title: Assistant Professor of Linguistics and Cognitive Science Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Cognitive Science Appl Deadline: 2026/08/31 23:59:59  (posted 2026/07/01 , updated 2026/06/24 , listed until 2026/09/30) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department\r\nof Linguistics and Cognitive Science at Pomona College invites applications\r\nfor two tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in Cognitive Science to\r\nbegin Fall 2027. We seek candidates in any area of specialization within\r\nCognitive Science that helps to inform theory, methods, and/or modeling in\r\ncognition, with the goal of uniquely expanding upon the existing strengths of\r\nthe current Cognitive Science faculty ( Lise Abrams and Megan Zirnstein ). The Cognitive\r\nScience major is diverse, interdisciplinary, and rapidly growing in student\r\ninterest. Therefore, we are particularly eager to see applications from an\r\nequally diverse pool of candidates who are outstanding scholars, dedicated\r\neducators, and enthusiastic about contributing to a vibrant, liberal arts\r\ncommunity. Candidates will provide evidence of a demonstrated\r\ncommitment to inclusive excellence in teaching and mentoring of undergraduates,\r\na productive record of research, and an ability to involve undergraduates in\r\nresearch and advise thesis projects. Professors are expected to maintain an\r\nactive research program along with a teaching load of two courses per semester.\r\nCourses to be taught include Introduction to Cognitive Science as well as new courses\r\nat the intermediate and advanced levels within the candidate's area(s) of\r\nexpertise, which will contribute to a core set of departmental classes for the\r\nCognitive Science major. The major is truly interdisciplinary, allowing\r\nstudents to take not only Cognitive Science courses but also Linguistics\r\ncourses within the department and courses in other departments such as\r\nPhilosophy, Computer Science, and Music. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. by the start of the\r\nposition, which is July 1, 2027. Please submit your application online at Academic Jobs Online #32240 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum\r\nvitae, (c) three 1-2 page statements – one describing teaching philosophy and\r\nexperience, one describing research including future directions, and one\r\ndescribing a demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (see Preparing\r\nyour Equity and Inclusion Statement ), (d) summary of teaching evaluations,\r\nand (e) up to three samples of published work or research in progress. You must\r\nalso provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt\r\nyou to enter your recommenders’ contact information. Review of applications will begin on September 1, 2026, and\r\nwill continue until the two positions are filled. Pomona College offers robust\r\ncompensation. The salary for this\r\nposition will range from $105,000 to $130,000 and will be set based on a\r\nvariety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience,\r\neducation, specialty, and training . Pomona College, the founding member of the Claremont\r\nColleges, is a highly selective liberal arts college attracting a diverse\r\ncommunity of approximately 1700 students. It is part of the Claremont\r\nConsortium, which includes the five Claremont Colleges, the Claremont Graduate\r\nUniversity, and the Keck Graduate Institute. For further information, please\r\ncontact Dr. Lise Abrams , chair of\r\nthe search committee. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Teaching Evaluations Diversity statement Three publications Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.pomona.edu/academics/departments/linguistics-cognitive-science 185 E. 6th Street 203 Edmunds Building Claremont, CA 91711 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32247": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, Politics Department Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, Politics Department Position ID: Pomona-Politics Department-AP [#32247] Position Title: Assistant Professor of Politics Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: American Politics Appl Deadline: 2026/09/29 23:59:59  (posted 2026/07/27 , listed until 2026/09/29) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Politics at Pomona College invites applications for an Assistant Professor in American Politics beginning Fall 2027. We are seeking candidates with expertise in Race and Ethnic Politics (REP) with a preference for candidates whose research and teaching interests complement those of the current faculty or who can contribute to expanding the curriculum. The successful candidate will regularly teach Introduction to American Politics and additional courses of their own design in their fields of expertise. We are especially looking for candidates who wish to advise and mentor undergraduates and whose teaching demonstrates commitment to inclusive excellence. The teaching load is two courses per semester, and professors are expected to maintain an active research program as well as participate in department and faculty governance.\r\nPlease submit your application online to Academic Jobs Online #32247 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) two, 1-2 page statements - one describing teaching philosophy and experience with inclusive pedagogy and one describing research, (d) teaching evaluations, (e) two sample syllabi, and (f) and one writing sample of published work or research in progress. You must also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information.\r\nTHE INSTITUTION: Pomona College is a highly selective liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1700 students, all undergraduates. We seek to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty who are committed to working with undergraduates. We welcome applications from candidates who can demonstrate a commitment to creating inclusive learning in their classrooms. Pomona College is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also include Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, Claremont Graduate University, and Keck Graduate Institute. Collectively, the Claremont Colleges constitute an academic community of approximately 8,000 students. As a result, Pomona students enjoy both the personalized instruction and close faculty-student interactions afforded by a small liberal arts college as well as the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona College is in Claremont, which is located east of Los Angeles and is accessible to the region’s major cities. \r\nQUALIFICATIONS: PhD in Political Science. Advanced doctoral students will also be considered.\r\nAPPLICATION PROCEDURES: Review of applications will begin on September 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be given full consideration. Pomona College offers a competitive compensation package commensurate with experience. The salary range for this position is $105,000-$125,000. Tenure-track faculty also receive a full year pre-tenure paid sabbatical. \r\nPlease contact the department chair, Professor Tom Le, tom.le@pomona.edu (tel. (909) 607-8202), with any questions you may have. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Graduate School Transcripts Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness (i.e. teaching evaluations) Two Sample Syllabi Teaching Statement (Statement of Inclusive Pedagogy+ Written Samples of Researchs Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.pomona.edu/academics/departments/politics/ 425 N. College Ave. Claremont, CA  91711 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32248": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, Psychology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, Psychology Position ID: Pomona-Psychology-AP [#32248] Position Title: Assistant Professor of Psychological Science & Neuroscience Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Psychology / Behavioral Neuroscience Neuroscience / Behavioral Appl Deadline: 2026/10/15 23:59:59  (posted 2026/07/01 , updated 2026/06/24 , listed until 2026/12/24) Position Description: Apply Position Description Pomona College Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Psychological Science and Neuroscience. The Departments of Psychological Science and Neuroscience seek candidates with a demonstrated commitment to teaching and the ability to establish an active research program that involves undergraduates. The position (75% appointment in Psychological Science and 25% appointment in Neuroscience) will begin in July 2027. The candidate is expected to teach two courses per semester, in addition to supervising senior theses. Courses may include introductory psychology and/or human neuroscience, as well as courses (elective, lab, or seminar) in the candidate’s area of research expertise. The successful candidate will have a productive research program examining the neural and/or physiological mechanisms of psychological phenomena. Pomona College is a selective liberal arts college, known for small classes, a challenging curriculum, and close relationships between students and faculty. The College has a generous pre-tenure sabbatical and offers internal support for faculty research, as well as professional development opportunities for faculty at all stages of their careers. The Departments of Psychological Science and Neuroscience are strongly committed to inclusive excellence in teaching and mentoring a diverse student body. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a related field. Please submit a dossier including (a) a cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) three brief statements – one addressing teaching philosophy and experience; one addressing scholarship, including future directions; and one addressing the candidate’s ability to mentor a diverse student body, (d) up to three representative reprints or pre-prints, and (e) three letters of recommendation. Candidates are welcome but not required to submit teaching evaluations (comprehensive information rather than selected comments are preferred). Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. All dossiers will be submitted to Academic Jobs Online [ https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32248 ; job #32248].  Questions should be referred to Professor Sara Masland (Sara.Masland@pomona.edu). Pomona College offers a robust compensation and benefits package. The salary range for this position is $105,000-$130,000. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Mentoring Statement Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.pomona.edu/academics/departments/psychology/ 909-607-3757 647 N. College Way Lincoln 2128 - Psychology Claremont, CA  91711 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32242": {
      "title": "AcademicJobsOnline.org",
      "description": "Pomona College, Sociology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Pomona College, Sociology Position ID: PomonaCollege-Sociology-AP [#32242] Position Title: Assistant Professor of Sociology Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Claremont, California 91711, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Social Sciences / Sociology Appl Deadline: 2026/09/10 23:59:59  (posted 2026/07/01 , updated 2026/06/24 , listed until 2026/09/10) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Sociology at Pomona College invites\r\napplications for an Assistant Professor to begin Fall 2027. Areas of specialization are open, but preference will be\r\ngiven to candidates who can teach sociology electives currently not offered by\r\nthe Claremont Colleges. Candidates are expected to rotate into teaching\r\nintroduction to sociology and/or the senior seminar. We also welcome candidates\r\nwho can teach qualitative research methods and/or classical sociological\r\ntheory.  The teaching load is two courses\r\nper semester. The candidate must have demonstrated the ability to maintain an\r\nactive, high-quality research program and the highest standards of excellence\r\nin teaching. For more information about the department, please visit our website. Pomona College is the founding\r\nmember of the Claremont Colleges (Claremont Graduate University, Claremont\r\nMcKenna, Harvey Mudd, Pitzer, Pomona, and Scripps), private, liberal arts\r\ncolleges located in the Los Angeles metropolitan area. Pomona College and the\r\nSociology Department support equal access to higher education. Of the students\r\nadmitted into the class of 2029, 55% self-identify as domestic students of\r\ncolor, 15% international and 24% are first generation college students. The\r\nsuccessful candidate will have demonstrated ability or potential to teach and\r\nmentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds—utilizing pedagogical\r\npractices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. in sociology by the\r\nstart of the position, which is July 1, 2027. Applications should include a\r\ncover letter; curriculum vitae; research statement; teaching statement that\r\naddresses the candidate's ability to teach, mentor, and inspire students\r\nrepresenting a broad range of backgrounds ; evidence of teaching effectiveness; and a writing sample. Please also provide\r\nthree letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter\r\nyour recommenders’ contact information. Complete applications will be reviewed beginning September 9,\r\n2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Dossiers\r\nshould be submitted to Academic Jobs Online #32242. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range\r\nfrom $105,000 to $125,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors,\r\nincluding but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty,\r\nand training . For further information, please contact Hung Cam Thai ,\r\nchair of the search committee. As an equal\r\nopportunity workplace, Pomona College is committed to fairness regardless of\r\nrace, color, ancestry, religion, sex, national origin, sexual orientation, age,\r\ncitizenship, ability, gender identity or Veteran status. Through its Strategic\r\nVision, Pomona College remains steadfast in its goal of creating workplace\r\nenvironments where all can flourish. The department welcomes applications from\r\nscholars who are ready to contribute to providing equitable access to education\r\nand expanding opportunities in the liberal arts and natural sciences. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching statement Scholarship statement Diversity statement Writing sample Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.pomona.edu/academics/departments/sociology 550 North College Avenue Claremont, CA 91711 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/general/faculty-jobs": {
      "title": "Tenure Track positions",
      "description": "Faculty Positions | Pomona College in Claremont, California - Pomona College Skip to main content Main Navigation Admissions & Aid Academics Life @ Pomona News & Events About Alumni & Families Give today Quicklinks & Search Menu Close Main navigation Admissions & Aid Academics Life @ Pomona News & Events About Alumni & Families Search Go Quicklinks Quicklinks A-Z Directory Athletics Campus Map Catalog Dining Menus Give Today Pages for... Audience Navigation New Students Students Faculty Staff Quicklinks & Search Search Go Quicklinks Quicklinks (Mobile) A-Z Directory Athletics Campus Map Catalog Dining Menus Give Today Pages for... Pages For… New Students Students Faculty Staff Close Menu You are here Home Administration Academic Dean's Office Information, General Policies & Forms Faculty Jobs Faculty Positions Tenure Track positions Assistant Professor of Biology The Department of Biology is hiring a tenure track assistant professor of biology to join the department in July 2027. We are seeking a plant biologist in any area of plant biology, however those with an interest in plant physiology are especially encouraged to apply. We are looking for candidates with a demonstrated interest in teaching and conducting research with undergraduate students. The successful candidate will offer an upper-level course in Plant Physiology or other upper-level plant biology course with laboratory, and teach an introductory course with laboratory. Pomona College is a private, undergraduate, selective liberal arts institution enrolling approximately 1,700 students. Pomona College is the oldest and founding member of the Claremont Colleges, a consortium of institutions that include five undergraduate colleges and two graduate universities, located in southern California. Intercollegiate collaboration among the colleges provides university-scale services and facilities, including the Bernard Field Station and the Claremont Colleges Libraries. While each institution is independent, undergraduate students can take courses at any of the colleges and research collaboration among faculty is encouraged through professional development networks. Pomona faculty in the Environmental Analysis Department partner regularly with colleagues in and beyond Claremont. The faculty in Biology welcome applicants who are committed to high quality, innovative undergraduate teaching and to mentoring undergraduate student research. Pomona College’s plant growth facility includes greenhouses, growth chambers, and a headhouse for planting, preparation, and storage, with a full-time dedicated greenhouse manager (more detailed information on the facilities is available). The College actively promotes a dynamic and inclusive environment in which students and employees of diverse backgrounds, cultures, and perspectives can learn and work. Pomona College is an equal opportunity employer that welcomes members of all groups and reaffirms its commitment not to discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, gender, age, religion, caste, gender identity or expression, disability, or sexual orientation in its educational programs, activities, and employment practices. In recent years, over 51% of entering classes identified as students of color, 23% as first-generation, 14% international, and nearly 20% as Pell eligible students. Recruiting and retaining a diverse faculty committed to building an inclusive community is vital to educating an equally diverse student body. Candidates who can contribute to this goal are particularly encouraged to apply. Preferred Qualifications : A Ph.D. in a field of biology by the start of employment and expertise in plant biology is required. Teaching experience; a demonstrated commitment to undergraduate teaching and research; and a commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, in biology are also priorities. Minimum Qualifications : Ph.D. in a field of biology by the start of employment and expertise in plant biology. Application Deadline: (at 11:59 PM Pacific Time): 10/01/2026 Open Until Filled: Yes Special Instructions to Applicants : Applicants should submit to Academic Jobs Online #32243 1) a cover letter; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a statement demonstrating commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, in environmental science; 4) a statement of teaching principles (3 pages maximum); 5) a research statement (3 pages maximum); and 6) graduate transcripts. As part of the application process, all candidates must provide contact information for at least three references who can submit a confidential letter of recommendation; however, only the references of short-listed candidates (those invited to a phone/Zoom interview) will be asked to submit a letter. All application materials should be posted by Thursday, October 1 at 11:59 pm PDT. You must meet the minimum qualifications to be considered for this position. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $130,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training For any questions, please contact Dr. E. J. Crane, ej.crane@pomona.edu . When will references be contacted to provide a letter of recommendation? When candidate is invited to phone interview. Assistant Professor of  Computer Science Pomona College seeks applications for an Assistant Professor of Computer Science to begin on July 1, 2027. We seek candidates with a strong commitment to i) inclusive undergraduate teaching across all levels of our curriculum; ii) mentoring a diverse student body and fostering a supportive learning environment; iii) developing a strong, independent research program that involves undergraduates and leads to peer-reviewed publications; and iv) helping build community within our Department and the College. We welcome applicants with broad intellectual interests from a wide range of subdisciplines, with a preference for candidates whose research and teaching interests complement those of the current faculty ( Computer Science Department Website ) or who can contribute to expanding the department's curricular range and scholarly interests. The teaching commitment is four courses per year over two semesters of instruction (2-2). CS faculty are expected to teach at least two required courses among our introductory (introduction to programming and computer science, mathematical foundations, and data structures) and/or core (algorithms, languages and theory of computation, and computer systems) sequences. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in hand by their start date, usually July 1, 2027. Our department values experience working with undergraduate students from diverse backgrounds and a commitment to improving higher education for underrepresented students through teaching, scholarship, or service. After graduation, recent CS majors have taken jobs in industry and national research labs and have pursued graduate programs at top research labs. Pomona College, located in Southern California, is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also includes Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, and Claremont Graduate University. Students can cross-register for courses at any of the colleges, which allows Pomona students to enjoy the advantages of personalized instruction and close faculty-student interaction combined with the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona's Computer Science department currently has 10 full-time, tenured or tenure-track faculty members, and there are over 30 CS faculty across the Claremont Colleges. In collaboration with the Claremont College Consortium, Pomona College offers a variety of professional development, mentorship, and networking opportunities for junior faculty, including the opportunity for entering faculty members to apply for a fully funded leave in their fourth year at the College. A complete application includes a cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts, three brief statements–one addressing the candidate’s teaching philosophy, one addressing the candidate’s scholarship, and one addressing the candidate’s ability to mentor a diverse student body , and three or more letters of reference, at least one of which evaluates the candidate's teaching. Please upload electronic copies of all materials to Academic Jobs Online #32241 . Review of applications will begin on October 4, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $135,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . For further information, we can be reached via email at cssearch@pomona.edu. Assistant Professor of English The English Department at Pomona College invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor to begin Fall 2027. At a moment when the department is in conversation about diversifying the major as well as building on its current strengths, we seek a colleague whose research focuses on US-Latinx literature and culture. We are open to various areas of expertise. Transnational, hemispheric, and/or comparative approaches welcome. Areas of interest may include but are not limited to: decolonial solidarity frameworks, environmental and digital humanities, migration, border and critical refugee studies, gender and sexuality, performance, aesthetics, global South, and considerations of the more-than-human. We seek to attract, develop, and retain faculty that contribute to the cultural, linguistic, experiential, and diverse curricular offerings of the department. The teaching load at Pomona College is two courses per semester. Faculty are expected to teach across all levels of the undergraduate curriculum and are eligible for generous teaching and research grants. Candidates should have Ph.D. in hand at time of appointment. Complete application includes: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) graduate transcripts, (d) three brief, 1-page statements – one addressing teaching philosophy and experience, one addressing scholarship and future research directions, and one addressing demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (e) three letters of recommendations (f) writing sample 20pp. Please apply through Academic Jobs Online #32246 . Review of applications begins October 15, 2026. Pomona College is a highly selective private liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1750 students, all undergraduates, and a student-faculty ratio of eight to one. The College and the Department support equal access to higher education and value working in a richly diverse environment. Of the students admitted into the Class of 2024, 58.8% self-identify as domestic students of color, 13.6% international and 20.7% are first generation college students. The successful candidate will have the ability to teach and mentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds–utilizing pedagogical practices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Pomona is the founding member of The Claremont Colleges, a unique constellation of five small undergraduate colleges and two graduate institutions located in beautiful Southern California near the foothills of the San Gabriel mountains, about 35 miles east of Los Angeles. Students of the Claremont Colleges can cross-register for courses at any of the colleges and have access to a shared library system containing over 2 million volumes. Pomona students thus enjoy the advantages of personalized instruction and close faculty-student interaction in a small liberal arts college setting, combined with the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $125,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. For any questions, please contact Dr. Cherene Sherrard-Johnson, Search Committee Chair and Department Chair, cherene.sherrard-johnson@pomona.edu . Assistant Professor of Linguistics and Cognitive Science The Department of Linguistics and Cognitive Science at Pomona College invites applications for two tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in Cognitive Science to begin Fall 2027. We seek candidates in any area of specialization within Cognitive Science that helps to inform theory, methods, and/or modeling in cognition, with the goal of uniquely expanding upon the existing strengths of the current Cognitive Science faculty ( Lise Abrams and Megan Zirnstein ). The Cognitive Science major is diverse, interdisciplinary, and rapidly growing in student interest. Therefore, we are particularly eager to see applications from an equally diverse pool of candidates who are outstanding scholars, dedicated educators, and enthusiastic about contributing to a vibrant, liberal arts community. Candidates will provide evidence of a demonstrated commitment to inclusive excellence in teaching and mentoring of undergraduates, a productive record of research, and an ability to involve undergraduates in research and advise thesis projects. Professors are expected to maintain an active research program along with a teaching load of two courses per semester. Courses to be taught include Introduction to Cognitive Science as well as new courses at the intermediate and advanced levels within the candidate's area(s) of expertise, which will contribute to a core set of departmental classes for the Cognitive Science major. The major is truly interdisciplinary, allowing students to take not only Cognitive Science courses but also Linguistics courses within the department and courses in other departments such as Philosophy, Computer Science, and Music. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. by the start of the position, which is July 1, 2027. Please submit your application online at Academic Jobs Online #32240 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) three 1-2 page statements – one describing teaching philosophy and experience, one describing research including future directions, and one describing a demonstrated ability to mentor a diverse student body (see Preparing your Equity and Inclusion Statement ), (d) summary of teaching evaluations, and (e) up to three samples of published work or research in progress. You must also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. Review of applications will begin on September 1, 2026, and will continue until the two positions are filled. Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $130,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . Pomona College, the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, is a highly selective liberal arts college attracting a diverse community of approximately 1700 students. It is part of the Claremont Consortium, which includes the five Claremont Colleges, the Claremont Graduate University, and the Keck Graduate Institute. For further information, please contact Dr. Lise Abrams, lise.abrams@pomona.edu , chair of the search committee. Assistant Professor of Politics (Race and Ethnic Politics) The Department of Politics at Pomona College invites applications for an Assistant Professor in American Politics beginning Fall 2027. We are seeking candidates with expertise in Race and Ethnic Politics (REP) with a preference for candidates whose research and teaching interests complement those of the current faculty or who can contribute to expanding the curriculum. The successful candidate will regularly teach Introduction to American Politics and additional courses of their own design in their fields of expertise. We are especially looking for candidates who wish to advise and mentor undergraduates and whose teaching demonstrates commitment to inclusive excellence. The teaching load is two courses per semester, and professors are expected to maintain an active research program as well as participate in department and faculty governance. Please submit your application online to Academic Jobs Online #32247 and include: (a) cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) two, 1-2 page statements - one describing teaching philosophy and experience with inclusive pedagogy and one describing research, (d) teaching evaluations, (e) two sample syllabi, and (f) and one writing sample of published work or research in progress. You must also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. The institution : Pomona College is a highly selective liberal arts college with an enrollment of approximately 1700 students, all undergraduates. We seek to attract, develop, and retain the highest quality faculty who are committed to working with undergraduates. We welcome applications from candidates who can demonstrate a commitment to creating inclusive learning in their classrooms. Pomona College is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also include Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, Claremont Graduate University, and Keck Graduate Institute. Collectively, the Claremont Colleges constitute an academic community of approximately 8,000 students. As a result, Pomona students enjoy both the personalized instruction and close faculty-student interactions afforded by a small liberal arts college as well as the intellectual and extracurricular resources typically found at much larger universities. Pomona College is in Claremont, which is located east of Los Angeles and is accessible to the region’s major cities. Qualifications : PhD in Political Science. Advanced doctoral students will also be considered. Application procedures : Review of applications will begin on September 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be given full consideration. Pomona College offers a competitive compensation package commensurate with experience. The salary range for this position is $105,000-$125,000; compensation is based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . Tenure-track faculty also receive a full year pre-tenure paid sabbatical. Please contact the department chair, Professor Tom Le, tom.le@pomona.edu (tel. (909) 607-8202), with any questions you may have. Assistant Professor of Psychological Science and Neuroscience The Departments of Psychological Science and Neuroscience seek candidates with a demonstrated commitment to teaching and the ability to establish an active research program that involves undergraduates. The position (75% appointment in Psychological Science and 25% appointment in Neuroscience) will begin in July 2027. The candidate is expected to teach two courses per semester, in addition to supervising senior theses. Courses may include introductory psychology and/or human neuroscience, as well as courses (elective, lab, or seminar) in the candidate’s area of research expertise. The successful candidate will have a productive research program examining the neural and/or physiological mechanisms of psychological phenomena. Pomona College is a selective liberal arts college, known for small classes, a challenging curriculum, and close relationships between students and faculty. The College has a generous pre-tenure sabbatical and offers internal support for faculty research, as well as professional development opportunities for faculty at all stages of their careers. The Departments of Psychological Science and Neuroscience are strongly committed to inclusive excellence in teaching and mentoring a diverse student body. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a related field. Please submit a dossier including (a) a cover letter, (b) curriculum vitae, (c) three brief statements – one addressing teaching philosophy and experience; one addressing scholarship, including future directions; and one addressing the candidate’s ability to mentor a diverse student body, (d) up to three representative reprints or pre-prints, and (e) three letters of recommendation. Candidates are welcome but not required to submit teaching evaluations (comprehensive information rather than selected comments are preferred). Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. All dossiers will be submitted to Academic Jobs Online #32248 . Questions should be referred to Professor Sara Masland ( Sara.Masland@pomona.edu ). Pomona College offers a robust compensation and benefits package. The salary range for this position is $105,000-130,000. Compensation is based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. Assistant Professor of Sociology The Department of Sociology at Pomona College invites applications for an Assistant Professor to begin Fall 2027. Areas of specialization are open, but preference will be given to candidates who can teach sociology electives currently not offered by the Claremont Colleges. Candidates are expected to rotate into teaching introduction to sociology and/or the senior seminar. We also welcome candidates who can teach qualitative research methods and/or classical sociological theory. The teaching load is two courses per semester. The candidate must have demonstrated the ability to maintain an active, high-quality research program and the highest standards of excellence in teaching. For more information about the department, please visit our sociology department website. Pomona College is the founding member of the Claremont Colleges (Claremont Graduate University, Claremont McKenna, Harvey Mudd, Pitzer, Pomona, and Scripps), private, liberal arts colleges located in the Los Angeles metropolitan area. Pomona College and the Sociology Department support equal access to higher education. Of the students admitted into the class of 2029, 55% self-identify as domestic students of color, 15% international and 24% are first generation college students. The successful candidate will have demonstrated ability or potential to teach and mentor exceptional students of diverse backgrounds—utilizing pedagogical practices that are rigorous, inclusive, and which promote academic equity. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. in sociology by the start of the position, which is July 1, 2027. Applications should include a cover letter; curriculum vitae; research statement; teaching statement that addresses the candidate's ability to teach, mentor, and inspire students representing a broad range of backgrounds ; evidence of teaching effectiveness; and a writing sample. Please also provide three letters of recommendation: the application site will prompt you to enter your recommenders’ contact information. Complete applications will be reviewed beginning September 9, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Dossiers should be submitted to Academic Jobs Online #32242 . Pomona College offers robust compensation. The salary for this position will range from $105,000 to $125,000 and will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training . For further information, please contact Hung Cam Thai, hung.thai@pomona.edu , chair of the search committee. As an equal opportunity workplace, Pomona College is committed to fairness regardless of race, color, ancestry, religion, sex, national origin, sexual orientation, age, citizenship, ability, gender identity or Veteran status. Through its Strategic Vision, Pomona College remains steadfast in its goal of creating workplace environments where all can flourish. The department welcomes applications from scholars who are ready to contribute to providing equitable access to education and expanding opportunities in the liberal arts and natural sciences. About Pomona College : Pomona College is a selective liberal arts college and the founding member of the Claremont Colleges, which also includes Claremont McKenna, Scripps, Pitzer, Harvey Mudd, Keck Graduate Institute, and Claremont Graduate University. Located in southern California, the Claremont Colleges constitute an academic community of 9,000 students and close to 4,000 faculty and staff. In collaboration with the Claremont Colleges Consortium, Pomona offers a variety of professional development, mentorship, and networking opportunities for faculty at all stages of their careers. Located near Los Angeles, California, Pomona College is widely regarded as one of the nation's premier liberal arts colleges. Established in 1887, Pomona College is known for small classes, a challenging curriculum, close relationships between students and faculty, and a range of student research and leadership opportunities. Academic Dean's Office Office A-Z Information, General Policies & Forms Faculty Jobs Leaves Faculty Committees Accreditation Access to Course Essentials (ACE) ITS Purchasing Visiting Scholars Travel Tracker Instructions Internal Funding for Research & Pedagogy Human Subjects Research (IRB) Sponsored Research Teaching, Learning & Advising Student Academic Success Faculty Development Resources for Chairs Statements/Messages Diversity Our Staff Contact Us Sabina Klemm Contracts and Hiring Specialist sabina.klemm@pomona.edu (909) 621-8518 Mailing Address Pomona College 333 N. College Way Claremont , CA 91711 Get in touch Contact (909) 621-8000 Contact Us Connect Social media  links Instagram Twitter Tiktok LinkedIn Youtube Facebook Footer The Arts Athletics Accessibility Location & Maps Emergency Employment Social Media Directory TCCS Give back to Pomona Support Pomona Part of The Claremont Colleges Legal ©2026 Pomona College Privacy Nondiscrimination Feedback"
    },
    "https://www.pomona.edu/administration/academic-dean/general/faculty-committees": {
